; SPDX-License-Identifier: 0BSD
; gridcard.source.vla - the grid card, as its author writes it: the test card's twin in
; grid mode, every kind of value, format and look a grid cell can hold, drawn by the
; viewport (ENGINE.8). The pass expands this file into gridcard.vla beside it, the rows
; the engine loads (docs/SPEC.md section 7.8): edit this one, then expand it again. The
; fixture of ENGINE.8's oracle: the page's loop oracle holds the window the host draws
; to the record of the same window at every frame, cell for cell, text, fill and place.
;
; What it shows, through a window of 80 by 50 cells, section 10's limit, on a Screen 96
; columns wide under a Camera that a person's edit moves, the viewport following it:
;   - the sixteen colors, each a number drawn through its look: the plane's Palette as
;     the grid's look map, one model with two renderers (docs/SPEC.md decision 19);
;   - a value of each kind a cell can hold, and the numbers as the record spells them;
;   - a formula computing each kind every frame, a number among them whose look, and so
;     whose fill, changes every frame;
;   - a text that spills into its empty neighbors and one its neighbor cuts; a number
;     under a text format; a wrapped text in a tall row; a cell's own style, and a look
;     over a style; a sentence row; a hidden row and a hidden column, each holding a
;     value that is never drawn; a narrow column and a column whose style fills its
;     empty cells; looks on a text, a truth value and an error;
;   - numbers under codes of Excel's, each drawn as the text the record prints for it
;     (KERNEL.47, drawn since CART.12), a formula's among them every frame, a text under
;     a code's text section, a code under a look of General, and a glyph through a look;
;   - and below them a stripe that moves a cell a frame, through two looks.
; A formula before its first value shows its text, so frame 0 shows the Screen's
; formulas as written.

(cartridge "gridcard"
  (spec 3)                          ; the version of docs/SPEC.md this cartridge is written to
  (title "Grid card")               ; what the host shows
  (rate 30)                         ; frames a second
  (screen grid 80 50)               ; the window: 80 cells wide and 50 high, section 10's limit, placed by the Camera
  (seed 1)                          ; the dice, which nothing here reads
  (author "Spreadsheet Company")    ; who wrote it
  (license "0BSD")                  ; the header's twin
  (notes "The grid card: every kind of value, format and look a grid cell can hold, drawn by the viewport, on a Screen 96 columns wide under a Camera that an edit moves. The sixteen colors are numbers drawn through their looks, a formula's value changes its fill every frame, and a stripe moves below."))

; The library whose macros this source calls: on, cells and rules, each naming a sheet
; once over a run of its rows; rule, a formula row from an expression; looks, a look map
; from a list of fills; and camera, a still Camera.
(include "../common.vla")

; The card's own macro: the CGA's sixteen through the library's look map, the plane's
; Palette as the grid's looks. A look map from a palette was this card's own macro until
; CART.4 wrote one too, and it went into the library as looks.

(defmacro (cga-looks sheet)
  "the CGA's sixteen as formats 1 to 16, and a look from each color index, 0 to 15, to its format"
  (looks sheet                      ; the sixteen fills, index 0 first
    "000000"                        ; 0, black
    "0000AA"                        ; 1, blue
    "00AA00"                        ; 2, green
    "00AAAA"                        ; 3, cyan
    "AA0000"                        ; 4, red
    "AA00AA"                        ; 5, magenta
    "AA5500"                        ; 6, brown
    "AAAAAA"                        ; 7, light gray
    "555555"                        ; 8, dark gray
    "5555FF"                        ; 9, light blue
    "55FF55"                        ; 10, light green
    "55FFFF"                        ; 11, light cyan
    "FF5555"                        ; 12, light red
    "FF55FF"                        ; 13, light magenta
    "FFFF55"                        ; 14, yellow
    "FFFFFF"))                      ; 15, white

; The sheets, in tab order: the Screen, and the Camera that places its window.
(sheet "Screen" visible)
(sheet "Camera" visible)

; The Camera (docs/SPEC.md section 3.8): the window's top row and left column, two values that
; nothing but a person's edit moves, so that the card holds still to be clicked, scrolled
; and edited as a sheet. (cell "Camera" "B2" 5) moves the window four columns right and the
; viewport scrolls by the move, the person's place inside the window kept; from column 18
; the window's right edge passes the Screen's last column, CR, and the columns past it are
; empty.
(camera                            ; a still Camera, its labels beside its values:
  1                                 ;   the window's top row
  1)                                ;   the window's left column, which an edit moves

; The looks (docs/SPEC.md section 7.3): the CGA's sixteen for the numbers 0 to 15, then the
; formats the other looks and the styles name, then the looks on a text, a truth value
; and an error. A cell whose value is a look's draws with the look's format in place of
; its own; an empty cell and a formula with no value match none.
(cga-looks "Screen")
(format 17 none text nowrap)        ; a number format of text, no fill: a number drawn on the left
(format 18 "F7F4FC" general wrap)   ; a wrapped text's
(format 19 "555555" general nowrap) ; the look of "#"
(format 20 "F3F3EE" general nowrap) ; the look of "."
(format 21 "E2D8F6" general nowrap) ; the look of "x"
(format 22 "D5F5D5" general nowrap) ; the look of TRUE
(format 23 "EEEEEE" general nowrap) ; the look of FALSE
(format 24 "FFD5D5" general nowrap) ; the look of two errors
(format 25 "FFF2CC" general nowrap) ; a cell's own style
(format 26 "E8F0FE" general nowrap) ; a column's style, which fills its empty cells
(on "Screen"                        ; the sheet every row below writes
  (look "#" 19)                     ; the stripe's mark
  (look "." 20)                     ; the stripe's ground
  (look "x" 21)                     ; a text
  (look true 22)                    ; a truth value
  (look false 23)                   ; the other
  (look (error "#N/A") 24)          ; an error
  (look (error "#DIV/0!") 24)       ; another, to the same format
  (gridlines on)                    ; the gridlines drawn
  (column "A" 40 shown none)        ; the labels' column, wide
  (column "R" 3 shown none)         ; a narrow column: a number in it is clipped
  (column "S" none hidden none)     ; a hidden column: its cell is never drawn
  (column "T" 24 shown 26)          ; a styled column: its empty cells take its fill
  (row 9 45 shown)                  ; a tall row, 45 points: the wrapped text's
  (row 12 none hidden))             ; a hidden row: its cells are never drawn

; Row 1: a text that spills into its empty neighbors to the right.
(cell "Screen" "A1" "The grid card: every kind of value, format and look a grid cell can hold, drawn by the viewport through a Camera that an edit moves")

; Rows 3 to 6, a label in column A and the cells after it.
(cells "Screen"                     ; the sheet every entry below writes
  ("A3" "the sixteen colors, each through its look")
  ("B3" 0) ("C3" 1) ("D3" 2) ("E3" 3) ("F3" 4) ("G3" 5) ("H3" 6) ("I3" 7)
  ("J3" 8) ("K3" 9) ("L3" 10) ("M3" 11) ("N3" 12) ("O3" 13) ("P3" 14) ("Q3" 15)
  ("A4" "a value of each kind")
  ("B4" "x")                        ; a text, through its look
  ("C4" "7")                        ; a text that spells a number: no number's look
  ("D4" "")                         ; the empty text
  ("E4" true)                       ; a truth value, through its look
  ("F4" false)                      ; the other
  ("G4" (error "#NULL!"))           ; the seven error values, in ERROR.TYPE's order
  ("H4" (error "#DIV/0!"))          ; through its look
  ("I4" (error "#VALUE!"))
  ("J4" (error "#REF!"))
  ("K4" (error "#NAME?"))
  ("L4" (error "#NUM!"))
  ("M4" (error "#N/A"))             ; through its look
  ("A5" "numbers as the record spells them")
  ("B5" 3.0000000000000004)         ; one unit in the last place from 3: no look
  ("C5" 1e-300)                     ; nearly 0: spelled with three hundred zeros, clipped
  ("D5" -0)                         ; negative zero, spelled -0, through the look of 0
  ("E5" 7.0)                        ; a whole number written with a point, through the look of 7
  ("F5" 1e300)                      ; spelled with three hundred digits, clipped
  ("G5" -12.5)                      ; a negative fraction
  ("H5" 1234567.89)                 ; wider than its cell, clipped and never spilled
  ("I5" 0.5)                        ; a fraction
  ("A6" "a formula of each kind, every frame"))
(rules "Screen"                                              ; the sheet every entry below writes
  ("B6" (mod Clock!$B$1 16))                                 ; a color that cycles: its look, so its fill, changes every frame
  ("C6" (* Clock!$B$1 16))                                   ; a number past the looks
  ("D6" (/ Clock!$B$1 2))                                    ; a fraction every other frame
  ("E6" (* 0.1 3 10))                                        ; a sum one unit in the last place from 3
  ("F6" (/ 1 0))                                             ; an error, through its look
  ("G6" "x")                                                 ; a text, through its look
  ("H6" (= 1 1))                                             ; a truth value, through its look
  ("I6" (sin Clock!$B$1))                                    ; a sine
  ("J6" (- Clock!$B$1))                                      ; a negative
  ("K6" (int (/ Clock!$B$1 2)))                              ; a whole number, through the looks while under 16
  ("L6" (choose (+ 1 (mod Clock!$B$1 3)) 2 "x" true))        ; a value whose kind cycles, each through its look
  ("M6" $A$2)                                                ; a reference to an empty cell, which is 0
  ("N6" (+ Screen.last!N6 1))                                ; a counter read through its own twin
  ("O6" (if (= (mod Clock!$B$1 2) 0) 9 0.5)))                ; 9, through its look, and 0.5 by turns

; Rows 7 to 15: texts, formats, styles, a sentence, the hidden row and column, the looks.
(cells "Screen"                     ; the sheet every entry below writes
  ("A7" "a text cut by its neighbor")
  ("B7" "this text stops where the next cell holds a value")
  ("C7" "stop")
  ("A8" "a number under a text format, then under general")
  ("B8" 42)                         ; drawn on the left, its style the text format
  ("C8" 42)                         ; drawn on the right
  ("A9" "a wrapped text in a tall row")
  ("B9" "wrapped in a tall row")
  ("A10" "a cell's own style, and a look over a style")
  ("B10" "styled")                  ; its own style's fill
  ("C10" 5)                         ; its own style, and the look of 5 in its place
  ("A11" "a sentence row, read by no rule")
  ("B11" "the program's row 11 wrote this cell")
  ("A12" "a hidden row")
  ("B12" 99)                        ; never drawn
  ("A13" "a narrow, a hidden and a styled column")
  ("R13" 12345)                     ; clipped by its narrow column
  ("S13" "hidden")                  ; never drawn
  ("T13" "a cell of a styled column")
  ("A14" "looks on a text, a truth value and an error")
  ("B14" "#") ("C14" ".") ("D14" true) ("E14" false) ("F14" (error "#N/A")) ("G14" "x")
  ("A15" "the stripe below moves a cell a frame through two looks"))
(on "Screen"                        ; the sheet every row below writes
  (style "B8" 17)                   ; the text format
  (style "B9" 18)                   ; the wrapped text's
  (style "B10" 25)                  ; a cell's own style
  (style "C10" 25)                  ; the same, under the look of 5
  (sentence "B11" 11))              ; the program row that wrote the cell

; Row 16: numbers under codes of Excel's (KERNEL.47). The record prints, after a value
; under a format holding a code, the text the value draws under it, and the viewport
; draws that text and computes nothing (drawn since CART.12): aligned and matched to a
; look by the value, too wide for its column as many # as fit.
(format 27 none "0.00" nowrap)                       ; two decimals
(format 28 none "#,##0.00" nowrap)                   ; the thousands grouped
(format 29 none "0.00;(0.00)" nowrap)                ; a negative's own section, drawn without its minus
(format 30 none "0%" nowrap)                         ; a hundredfold
(format 31 "E2D8F6" "0;-0;0;\"text: \"@" nowrap)     ; a text's section, the fourth, and a fill
(format 32 "F7F4FC" "◆" nowrap)                      ; a glyph alone, whatever the number: a look's
(cells "Screen"                     ; the sheet every entry below writes
  ("A16" "numbers under codes of Excel's, each drawn as the record's text")
  ("B16" 1.5)                       ; 1.50
  ("C16" 2.675)                     ; 2.68, the decimal rounded as Excel rounds it, where the binary's is 2.67
  ("D16" 1234.5)                    ; 1,234.50
  ("E16" -3)                        ; (3.00)
  ("F16" 0.25)                      ; 25%
  ("H16" 1234567.891)               ; 1,234,567.89, wider than its cell: as many # as fit
  ("I16" "quanta")                  ; text: quanta, on the left, in its fill
  ("J16" 3)                         ; under 0.00, and the look of 3, General, in its place: 3, no text row
  ("K16" 0.125))                    ; the look of 0.125, a glyph's format: ◆
(rule "Screen" "G16" (/ Clock!$B$1 4))     ; a value under 0.00 every frame: 0.25, 0.50, 0.75, then 1 through
                                           ; the look of 1, General, its text row gone until 1.25
(on "Screen"                        ; the sheet every row below writes
  (style "B16" 27)                  ; two decimals
  (style "C16" 27)
  (style "D16" 28)                  ; the thousands grouped
  (style "E16" 29)                  ; a negative's own section
  (style "F16" 30)                  ; a hundredfold
  (style "G16" 27)                  ; the formula's
  (style "H16" 28)                  ; too wide
  (style "I16" 31)                  ; a text's section
  (style "J16" 27)                  ; under the look of 3
  (look 0.125 32))                  ; the glyph's look

; Rows 17 to 50: a stripe that moves, one fill over the Screen's whole width, a "#" every
; eighth cell along a diagonal and a "." elsewhere, each through its look.
(rule "Screen" "A17:CR50" (if (= (mod (+ (column) (row) Clock!$B$1) 8) 0) "#" "."))
